<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  MIT 201 - Statistical Computing I                                -->
<!--  Chapter: Descriptive Statistics and Data Summarisation           -->
<!--                                                                   -->
<!--  Data-first chapter. The motivating failure in Section 1 is the   -->
<!--  spine of the whole chapter; later sections refer back to it.     -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-descriptive-statistics-old" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Descriptive Statistics and Data Summarisation</title>

  <introduction xml:id="ch-descriptive-introduction">
    <p>
      A dataset of three hundred observations cannot be held in the mind all at
      once. To reason about it at all we must compress it into a handful of
      numbers, and the whole of descriptive statistics is the study of how to do
      that compression well.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter approaches the subject from the data rather than from the
      formulas. We begin with a real question about a real dataset, answer it
      with the most obvious summary available, and find that the answer is
      badly wrong. Everything that follows <mdash /> central tendency,
      dispersion, quantiles, frequency tables, grouped summaries, and the
      treatment of missing values <mdash /> is developed as a response to that
      failure. The formulas make far more sense once you have seen what goes
      wrong without them.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="sec-descriptive-in-r" /> is a reference section. It collects
      the R implementation of everything developed in the earlier sections, and
      may be read alongside them, after them, or skipped by a reader interested
      only in the concepts.
    </p>

    <note xml:id="note-dataset-provenance">
      <title>The dataset used throughout</title>
      <p>
        All examples in this chapter use <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c>, which
        records measurements on 344 flamingos from three Rift Valley soda
        lakes.
      </p>
      <p>
        This dataset is <em>synthetic</em>. The differences between the two
        species in size and bill shape are grounded in real flamingo biology,
        and the lake compositions reflect documented patterns, but the
        individual measurements were generated rather than observed. It exists
        for teaching. It should not be cited as evidence about real flamingo
        populations.
      </p>
    </note>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: the data-first hook                                   -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-flamingo-question">
    <title>A Question About Flamingos</title>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-three-lakes">
      <title>Three lakes, two species, 344 birds</title>

      <p>
        Kenya's Rift Valley holds a chain of shallow, strongly alkaline soda
        lakes. The alkalinity is hostile to most life but ideal for the
        cyanobacteria and diatoms that flamingos filter-feed on, which is why
        these lakes support some of the largest flamingo congregations
        anywhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two species share them. The <term>lesser flamingo</term> is the smaller
        of the two, with a deep, sharply decurved bill adapted to filtering
        cyanobacteria from the water column. The <term>greater flamingo</term>
        is considerably larger, with a longer and shallower bill, and feeds
        more on invertebrates in the sediment.
      </p>

      <p>
        A field team has measured 344 birds across Lakes Nakuru, Bogoria and
        Elementaita. Each row of the dataset describes one bird.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-flamingo-variables">
        <title>Variables recorded for each bird in <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Variable</cell>
            <cell>Type</cell>
            <cell>Description</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell><c>lake</c></cell><cell>categorical</cell><cell>Nakuru, Bogoria or Elementaita</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>species</c></cell><cell>categorical</cell><cell>Greater Flamingo or Lesser Flamingo</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>bill_length_mm</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Bill length in millimetres</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>bill_depth_mm</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Bill depth at the base, in millimetres</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>leg_length_mm</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Tarsus length in millimetres</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>body_mass_g</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Body mass in grams</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>sex</c></cell><cell>categorical</cell><cell>male or female</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>year</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Year of measurement (2021<ndash />2023)</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Load the file and look at the first few rows.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        flamingos &lt;- read.csv("kenya_flamingos.csv", stringsAsFactors = TRUE)
        head(flamingos)

        #&gt;          lake          species bill_length_mm bill_depth_mm leg_length_mm
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo          101.2          25.9         265.1
        #&gt; 2      Nakuru Greater Flamingo          135.8          22.5         371.8
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo           96.1          26.9         278.0
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Greater Flamingo          131.1          25.7         360.4
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo           94.1          24.3         264.1
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo           98.4          25.9         272.3
        #&gt;   body_mass_g    sex year
        #&gt; 1        1330 female 2022
        #&gt; 2        2841 female 2023
        #&gt; 3        1646   male 2022
        #&gt; 4        3476   male 2022
        #&gt; 5        1620 female 2021
        #&gt; 6        1963   male 2022
      </program>

      <p>
        Six rows is manageable. All 344 is not. This is the practical problem
        descriptive statistics exists to solve: we need to compress the dataset
        into a few numbers we can reason about. The remainder of this section
        does that compression badly, on purpose.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-first-question">
      <title>A first, obvious question</title>

      <p>
        <em>How big is a flamingo in the Rift Valley?</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the sort of question a conservation officer or a field
        ecologist might genuinely ask. The instinctive move is to take the
        average body mass.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mean(flamingos$body_mass_g)
        #&gt; [1] NA
      </program>

      <p>
        Not what we expected. <c>R</c> has returned <c>NA</c>, meaning <q>not
        available</q>, because some birds in the dataset have no recorded mass
        and R refuses to quietly pretend otherwise. This is deliberate design,
        and <xref ref="sec-missing-data" /> treats it properly. For now we tell
        R to set the missing values aside.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mean(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1957.405
      </program>

      <p>
        So the average Rift Valley flamingo weighs about
        1957 g, just under two kilograms. That is our answer, and it is arithmetically
        correct.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-something-wrong">
      <title>Something is wrong</title>

      <p>
        Let us check how the birds sit relative to this average. How many of
        them weigh less than 1957 g?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mass &lt;- flamingos$body_mass_g

        sum(mass &lt; 1957.405, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 253

        sum(!is.na(mass))    # birds that actually have a recorded mass
        #&gt; [1] 336
      </program>

      <observation xml:id="obs-three-quarters">
        <title>The problem</title>
        <p>
          253 of the 336 measured birds, <em>75.3%</em>, weigh less than the
          average bird. Three quarters of the flamingos are lighter than the
          average flamingo.
        </p>
      </observation>

      <p>
        Pause on that before reading on. Nothing has gone wrong with the
        arithmetic: <c>mean()</c> computed exactly what it is defined to
        compute. So what has gone wrong?
      </p>

      <p>
        The instinct at this point is to reach for a different function
        <mdash /> perhaps the median is <q>more correct</q>. Let us try.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        median(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1758.5
      </program>

      <p>
        1758.5 g, which is 199 g lower, about 11% below the mean. That is a
        substantial disagreement between two numbers that both claim to
        describe the same thing. Swapping one function for another has not
        resolved anything; it has produced a second answer with no obvious
        reason to prefer it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The problem is not the function. <em>The problem is the question.</em>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shape-of-data">
      <title>Looking at the shape of the data</title>

      <p>
        Rather than computing yet another summary, let us look at how the
        masses are actually distributed. Group them into 500 g bands, labelled
        in kilograms, and count.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        # Group into 500 g bands, labelled in kilograms
        bands &lt;- cut(mass, breaks = seq(1000, 4000, by = 500),
                     labels = c("1.0-1.5", "1.5-2.0", "2.0-2.5",
                                "2.5-3.0", "3.0-3.5", "3.5-4.0"))
        table(bands)

        #&gt; bands
        #&gt; 1.0-1.5 1.5-2.0 2.0-2.5 2.5-3.0 3.0-3.5 3.5-4.0
        #&gt;      50     209      15      20      34       8
      </program>

      <p>
        Read the counts across: 50, then 209, then a collapse to
        <term>15</term>, then back up to 20, 34 and 8.
      </p>

      <p>
        That dip is not noise. The data has two separate concentrations of mass
        <mdash /> a large one below 2000 g and a smaller one above 2500 g
        <mdash /> with a near-empty valley between them. A distribution with
        two peaks is called <term>bimodal</term><idx><h>bimodal</h></idx>, and
        it is precisely where our average landed: 1957 g sits on the shoulder
        of the lower group, pointing into the gap.
      </p>

      <p>
        The mean is the balance point of the data. When the data has two
        clusters, the balance point falls between them, describing neither.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-resolution">
      <title>The resolution</title>

      <p>
        We already know something that explains this. There are two species in
        the dataset.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        table(flamingos$species)

        #&gt; Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;               66              278

        tapply(mass, flamingos$species, mean, na.rm = TRUE)

        #&gt; Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;         3120.190         1689.070
      </program>

      <p>
        There is the answer. Greater flamingos average about 3120 g; lesser
        flamingos about 1689 g. These are not slightly different populations
        <mdash /> the greater flamingo is nearly twice the mass of the lesser.
        Because lesser flamingos outnumber greaters roughly four to one, the
        pooled mean is dragged toward the lesser group but not all the way,
        landing at 1957 g.
      </p>

      <p>
        And 1957 g describes no bird in this dataset in any useful sense.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>It is heavier than 91% of the lesser flamingos.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            It is lighter than <em>every single</em> greater flamingo. The
            lightest greater in the dataset is 2347 g.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Our summary statistic described a bird that does not exist: too heavy
        to be a typical lesser, too light to be any greater at all.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-what-went-wrong">
        <title>What actually went wrong</title>
        <p>
          The question <q>how big is a flamingo?</q> presupposes that there is
          one kind of flamingo. The dataset contains two.
        </p>
        <p>
          No choice of summary function could have fixed this, because the
          error was not in the arithmetic. It lay in applying a
          single-population summary to a two-population dataset. The mean
          answered faithfully; we asked badly.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-what-descriptive-statistics-is">
      <title>What descriptive statistics actually is</title>

      <p>
        It is tempting to define descriptive statistics as the set of functions
        that summarise data. That definition is what leads students to memorise
        <c>mean()</c>, <c>median()</c> and <c>sd()</c> and then misuse all
        three.
      </p>

      <p>
        A better definition: <em>a summary statistic is a deliberate decision
        about what information to throw away.</em> The mean discards everything
        except the balance point. The median discards everything except the
        middle rank. Each is useful precisely because it discards; a summary
        that kept all the information would just be the dataset again.
      </p>

      <p>
        The skill, therefore, is not computing the summaries <mdash /> R
        computes them for you. The skill is judging which information you can
        afford to lose, and recognising when you have lost something that
        mattered.
      </p>

      <p>
        This section leaves us with three questions, which structure the rest
        of the chapter.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-chapter-roadmap">
        <title>Questions raised by <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" />, and where they are answered</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Question</cell>
            <cell>Answered in</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Which single number, if any, represents a <q>typical</q> value, and how do the candidates differ?</cell>
            <cell><xref ref="sec-central-tendency" /></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>How do we describe how spread out the data is, and spread around what?</cell>
            <cell><xref ref="sec-dispersion" /></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>How do we detect that a whole-dataset summary is misleading, ideally before it misleads us?</cell>
            <cell><xref ref="sec-quantiles" /> through <xref ref="sec-grouped-summaries" /></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 2: central tendency                                      -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-central-tendency">
    <title>Measures of Central Tendency</title>

    <p>
      A measure of central tendency<idx><h>central tendency</h></idx> answers
      the question: if I had to pick one value to stand for this whole
      variable, what would it be? There are three standard answers, and they
      disagree in informative ways.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mean">
      <title>The mean</title>

      <p>
        The arithmetic mean<idx><h>mean</h></idx> of <m>n</m> observations is
        their total divided by their count:
      </p>

      <me>\bar{x} = \frac{x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_n}{n} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i</me>

      <p>
        Every observation contributes. That is the mean's strength <mdash /> it
        uses all the information <mdash /> and equally its weakness: a single
        extreme value can move it substantially, and it has no defence against
        the bimodality we met in <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" />.
        Physically, it is the balance point of the distribution.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-median">
      <title>The median</title>

      <p>
        Sort the values and take the middle one. With an even number of
        observations, average the middle two.
      </p>

      <p>
        The median<idx><h>median</h></idx> depends only on the <em>rank
        order</em>, not on the magnitudes. Doubling the heaviest bird in the
        dataset would not move it at all. This makes the median
        <term>resistant</term> to outliers and to skew, at the cost of ignoring
        most of the information in the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mode">
      <title>The mode</title>

      <p>
        The most frequently occurring value. The mode<idx><h>mode</h></idx> has
        two properties the others lack, and one serious limitation.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            It is the <em>only</em> measure of central tendency available for
            genuinely categorical data. There is no arithmetic mean of
            <c>lake</c>, but there is a most common lake.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            It is the only one that can report more than one centre, which is
            exactly what our bimodal mass distribution needed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For continuous data it is ill-defined. In our dataset almost every
            mass is unique, so <q>the most frequent value</q> is close to
            meaningless until the data is binned or a smooth density is
            estimated.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Base R has no <c>mode()</c> function for this purpose. Confusingly,
        <c>mode()</c> does exist, but it reports a variable's storage type. For
        categorical data the idiom is:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        names(sort(table(flamingos$lake), decreasing = TRUE))[1]
        #&gt; [1] "Nakuru"
      </program>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-choosing-central">
      <title>Choosing between them</title>

      <table xml:id="table-choosing-central-tendency">
        <title>When each measure of central tendency is appropriate</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Measure</cell>
            <cell>Use when</cell>
            <cell>Avoid when</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Mean</cell>
            <cell>Data is roughly symmetric and unimodal; you need a value that uses all observations; you will do further arithmetic with it</cell>
            <cell>Strong skew, outliers, or more than one cluster</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Median</cell>
            <cell>Data is skewed or contains outliers; you want a robust typical value</cell>
            <cell>You need a statistic reflecting magnitudes, not just ranks</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Mode</cell>
            <cell>Data is categorical, or you need to detect multiple centres</cell>
            <cell>Data is continuous and unbinned</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <remark xml:id="remark-mean-median-gap">
        <title>The mean<ndash />median gap is a diagnostic</title>
        <p>
          For our flamingo masses the mean is 1957.4 and the median 1758.5. The
          mean exceeds the median by 199 g.
        </p>
        <p>
          As a rule of thumb, a mean noticeably greater than the median
          indicates a right-skewed distribution: a tail of large values pulling
          the balance point up. A mean below the median indicates left skew. A
          mean approximately equal to the median is consistent with symmetry.
        </p>
        <p>
          Comparing the two costs nothing and is among the cheapest warning
          signs available. But note its limits. It tells you <em>that</em> the
          distribution is asymmetric, not <em>why</em>. In our case the cause
          was two species, and only looking at the distribution revealed that.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 3: dispersion                                            -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-dispersion">
    <title>Measures of Dispersion</title>

    <p>
      Knowing the centre is not enough. Two lakes could have identical mean
      masses while one holds birds of near-uniform size and the other a mixture
      of extremes. Dispersion<idx><h>dispersion</h></idx> measures how spread
      out the values are.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-range-iqr">
      <title>Range and interquartile range</title>

      <p>
        The <term>range</term> is the largest value minus the smallest. It is
        trivial to compute and almost always inadequate: it uses exactly two
        observations and discards the rest, and it can only grow as more data
        is collected, which makes it unstable across samples of different
        sizes. Note that R's <c>range()</c> returns the two endpoints rather
        than the difference.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        range(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1206 3982
      </program>

      <p>
        The <term>interquartile range</term><idx><h>interquartile range</h></idx>
        is the width of the middle half of the data: the third quartile minus
        the first. Like the median it is rank-based and resistant to outliers,
        and it is the natural companion to the median.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        IQR(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 384
      </program>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-variance">
      <title>Variance</title>

      <p>
        Variance<idx><h>variance</h></idx> measures spread as the average
        squared distance from the mean:
      </p>

      <me>s^2 = \frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(x_i - \bar{x}\right)^2</me>

      <p>
        Two features of this formula deserve explanation.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Why square the deviations?</em> Because the raw deviations from the
        mean always sum to exactly zero <mdash /> that is what <q>balance
        point</q> means <mdash /> so their average carries no information.
        Squaring makes every contribution positive. It also weights large
        deviations disproportionately, which is either a feature or a liability
        depending on the data.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Why divide by <m>n-1</m> rather than <m>n</m>?</em> Because we are
        almost always estimating the variance of a population from a sample,
        and the sample mean is itself estimated from the same data. The
        observations are, on average, slightly closer to their own sample mean
        than to the true population mean, so dividing by <m>n</m> would
        systematically underestimate. Dividing by <m>n-1</m> corrects this. The
        quantity <m>n-1</m> is the <term>degrees of
        freedom</term><idx><h>degrees of freedom</h></idx>: once you know the
        mean and any <m>n-1</m> of the observations, the last one is
        determined.
      </p>

      <p>
        R's <c>var()</c> uses the <m>n-1</m> divisor by default, which is what
        you want for sample data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-standard-deviation">
      <title>Standard deviation</title>

      <p>
        Variance has one awkward property: its units are the square of the
        original units. The variance of our masses is 362504 <m>g^2</m>, which
        is not an interpretable quantity. Taking the square root returns us to
        grams.
      </p>

      <me>s = \sqrt{s^2}</me>

      <program language="r">
        var(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 362503.5

        sd(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 602.0827
      </program>

      <p>
        So the standard deviation<idx><h>standard deviation</h></idx> is about
        602 g, a typical distance from the mean expressed in grams. This is why
        the standard deviation, and not the variance, is what gets reported in
        practice.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-dispersion-around-what">
      <title>Dispersion around what?</title>

      <p>
        Now apply the lesson of <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" />. The
        standard deviation of 602 g measures spread around 1957 g, a value we
        established describes no bird. What happens if we compute it within
        each species instead?
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-pooled-vs-within">
        <title>Body mass dispersion, pooled and within species</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Group</cell>
            <cell>n</cell>
            <cell>Mean (g)</cell>
            <cell>SD (g)</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>All birds pooled</cell><cell>336</cell><cell>1957.4</cell><cell>602.1</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Greater Flamingo</cell><cell>63</cell><cell>3120.2</cell><cell>313.9</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell><cell>273</cell><cell>1689.1</cell><cell>196.4</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The pooled standard deviation is roughly two to three times the
        within-species figures. That excess is not biological variation among
        flamingos. It is the gap between two species, misreported as
        variability within one.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="remark-dispersion-centre">
        <title>Dispersion inherits the error in the centre</title>
        <p>
          A dispersion measure is only meaningful relative to the centre it is
          measured around. If that centre is wrong, the dispersion inherits the
          error and amplifies it.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 4: quantiles                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-quantiles">
    <title>Quantiles and Position</title>

    <p>
      Quantiles<idx><h>quantile</h></idx> generalise the median. The
      <m>p</m>-th quantile is the value below which a proportion <m>p</m> of
      the data falls. The median is the <m>0.5</m> quantile;
      <term>quartiles</term> cut the data at <m>0.25</m>, <m>0.5</m> and
      <m>0.75</m>; <term>percentiles</term> express the same idea on a
      <m>0</m><ndash /><m>100</m> scale.
    </p>

    <program language="r">
      quantile(mass, probs = c(0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1), na.rm = TRUE)

      #&gt;      0%     25%     50%     75%    100%
      #&gt; 1206.00 1570.25 1758.50 1954.25 3982.00
    </program>

    <p>
      These five numbers are the <term>five-number summary</term>, and together
      they carry considerably more information than a mean and a standard
      deviation. Read them here:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The middle half of all birds lies between 1570 g and 1954 g, a band
          just 384 g wide.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          But the maximum is 3982 g, more than 2000 g above the third quartile.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      That asymmetry <mdash /> a tight middle with a long reach on one side
      only <mdash /> is the signature of a right-skewed or multi-cluster
      distribution, visible without plotting anything. R's <c>summary()</c>
      gives these together with the mean.
    </p>

    <program language="r">
      summary(mass)

      #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.    NA's
      #&gt;    1206    1570    1758    1957    1954    3982       8
    </program>

    <p>
      Note that <c>summary()</c> reports the count of missing values as a
      matter of course. Reading that number is not optional; see
      <xref ref="sec-missing-data" />.
    </p>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 5: visualisation                                         -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-seeing-shape">
    <title>Seeing the Shape of a Distribution</title>

    <p>
      Every numeric summary in the preceding three sections either missed the
      two-species structure or, at best, hinted at it. The binned table in
      <xref ref="ss-shape-of-data" /> revealed it immediately. That is not a
      coincidence: numeric summaries compress, and plots do not. Where a
      summary can hide a feature, a well-chosen plot generally cannot.
    </p>

    <p>
      Four plot types cover most descriptive work.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-histogram">
      <title>Histogram</title>

      <p>
        A histogram<idx><h>histogram</h></idx> bins a continuous variable and
        draws the counts as bars, the graphical form of the table we built in
        <xref ref="ss-shape-of-data" />. This is the primary tool for seeing
        <term>modality</term> and <term>skew</term>.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        hist(mass, breaks = 20,
             main = "Body mass of Rift Valley flamingos",
             xlab = "Body mass (g)")
      </program>

      <p>
        The one parameter that matters is the bin width. Too wide and the two
        peaks merge into one; too narrow and every bar becomes noise. Always
        try several before drawing conclusions. A histogram is an argument, and
        the bin width is a choice you are making within that argument.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-boxplot">
      <title>Boxplot</title>

      <p>
        A boxplot<idx><h>boxplot</h></idx> draws the five-number summary as a
        box, from the first to the third quartile with the median marked, plus
        whiskers, flagging distant points individually. Boxplots are unmatched
        for comparing groups side by side.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        boxplot(body_mass_g ~ species, data = flamingos,
                ylab = "Body mass (g)")
      </program>

      <warning xml:id="warning-boxplot-modality">
        <title>A boxplot cannot show modality</title>
        <p>
          A boxplot is built entirely from quantiles. A single boxplot of our
          pooled body mass would show a box, a median line and some high points
          flagged as outliers, and would give no indication whatsoever that the
          data contains two distinct species.
        </p>
        <p>
          Boxplots are excellent for comparing groups you have <em>already
          identified</em>. They are poor for discovering that groups exist. If
          you have not yet plotted a histogram or a density curve, do not trust
          a boxplot to have told you the whole story.
        </p>
      </warning>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-density-scatter">
      <title>Density and scatter plots</title>

      <p>
        A <term>density plot</term> is a smoothed estimate of the distribution,
        avoiding the arbitrariness of bin edges. It is useful for overlaying
        groups on common axes.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>scatter plot</term> shows the joint behaviour of two numeric
        variables. It is the only one of the four that addresses
        <em>relationships</em> rather than a single variable's distribution.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-which-plot">
        <title>Matching the question to the plot</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Question you are asking</cell>
            <cell>Plot to reach for</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>Is this variable skewed? Does it have more than one peak?</cell><cell>Histogram, or density plot</cell></row>
          <row><cell>How do these groups compare in centre and spread?</cell><cell>Boxplot, grouped</cell></row>
          <row><cell>How are these two numeric variables related?</cell><cell>Scatter plot</cell></row>
          <row><cell>How do counts compare across categories?</cell><cell>Bar chart of a frequency table</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 6: frequency and cross-tabulation                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-frequency-tables">
    <title>Frequency Distributions and Cross-Tabulation</title>

    <p>
      Frequency tables<idx><h>frequency table</h></idx> count occurrences. For
      a single categorical variable this is straightforward.
    </p>

    <program language="r">
      table(flamingos$lake)

      #&gt;     Bogoria Elementaita      Nakuru
      #&gt;         124          68         152
    </program>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-cross-tabulation">
      <title>Cross-tabulation</title>

      <p>
        A <term>cross-tabulation</term><idx><h>cross-tabulation</h></idx>, or
        contingency table, counts the joint occurrences of two categorical
        variables. It is the standard tool for asking whether they are
        associated.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species)

        #&gt;               Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;   Bogoria                   10              114
        #&gt;   Elementaita               30               38
        #&gt;   Nakuru                    26              126
      </program>

      <p>
        Raw counts are hard to compare when the row totals differ; Nakuru has
        more than twice as many birds as Elementaita. Converting to <term>row
        proportions</term>, so that each row sums to one, makes the pattern
        legible.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        prop.table(table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species), margin = 1)

        #&gt;               Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;   Bogoria                0.081            0.919
        #&gt;   Elementaita            0.441            0.559
        #&gt;   Nakuru                 0.171            0.829
      </program>

      <p>
        Bogoria is overwhelmingly lesser flamingo, about 92%, while Elementaita
        is close to an even split at 44 to 56. Species composition varies
        substantially by lake, which is consistent with what is known about
        these lakes: the more strongly alkaline waters favour the cyanobacteria
        that lesser flamingos specialise in.
      </p>

      <p>
        The choice of <c>margin</c> encodes a different question.
        <c>margin = 1</c> gives row proportions, answering <q>of the birds at
        this lake, what fraction are each species?</q> <c>margin = 2</c> gives
        column proportions, answering <q>of this species, what fraction are at
        each lake?</q> These are different questions with different answers.
        Decide which you mean before you compute.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-binning">
      <title>Binning continuous variables</title>

      <warning xml:id="warning-tabulate-categorical">
        <title>Cross-tabulate categorical variables, not continuous ones</title>
        <p>
          Applying <c>table()</c> to a continuous variable such as
          <c>body_mass_g</c> produces a row for nearly every distinct value,
          most with a count of one. The result is not a frequency distribution.
          It is the original data, rearranged and harder to read.
        </p>
        <p>
          Continuous variables must be <term>binned</term> first, using
          <c>cut()</c>, which converts them into an ordered categorical
          variable. That is exactly what <xref ref="ss-shape-of-data" /> did.
        </p>
        <p>
          The choice of bin boundaries is a genuine analytical decision, not a
          formatting detail. Different boundaries can hide or reveal structure.
          As with histogram bin widths, try several.
        </p>
      </warning>

      <p>
        Binning in practice:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        flamingos$mass_band &lt;- cut(
          flamingos$body_mass_g,
          breaks = c(1000, 1500, 2000, 2500, 3000, 3500, 4000),
          labels = c("1.0-1.5", "1.5-2.0", "2.0-2.5",
                     "2.5-3.0", "3.0-3.5", "3.5-4.0"))

        table(flamingos$mass_band, flamingos$species)

        #&gt;           Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;   1.0-1.5                0               50
        #&gt;   1.5-2.0                0              209
        #&gt;   2.0-2.5                1               14
        #&gt;   2.5-3.0               20                0
        #&gt;   3.0-3.5               34                0
        #&gt;   3.5-4.0                8                0
      </program>

      <p>
        This single table contains the whole argument of
        <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" />. The two species occupy almost
        entirely separate mass bands, overlapping only in the 2.0<ndash />2.5 kg
        row, where just one greater flamingo meets fourteen lessers. A pooled
        average across these two columns was never going to describe anything
        real.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 7: grouped summaries                                     -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-grouped-summaries">
    <title>Grouped Summaries</title>

    <p>
      The formal resolution to <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" /> is to stop
      summarising the dataset as a whole and start summarising <em>within
      groups</em>. The pattern is called
      <term>split<ndash />apply<ndash />combine</term><idx><h>split-apply-combine</h></idx>:
      split the data by a grouping variable, apply a summary to each piece,
      combine the results into a table.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-grouped-species">
      <title>Body mass by species</title>
      <tabular halign="left">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>species</cell><cell>n</cell><cell>mean (g)</cell>
          <cell>sd (g)</cell><cell>median (g)</cell><cell>IQR (g)</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Greater Flamingo</cell><cell>63</cell><cell>3120.2</cell><cell>313.9</cell><cell>3102.0</cell><cell>326.5</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell><cell>273</cell><cell>1689.1</cell><cell>196.4</cell><cell>1684.0</cell><cell>307.0</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Two things are now visible that the pooled summary destroyed. First, the
      species differ enormously in mean mass. Second, and less obvious, within
      each species the mean and median now agree closely: 3120 against 3102,
      and 1689 against 1684. The skew we detected in
      <xref ref="sec-central-tendency" /> was <em>entirely</em> an artefact of
      pooling. Neither species is individually skewed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Grouping can use more than one variable.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-grouped-lake-species">
      <title>Mean body mass by lake and species</title>
      <tabular halign="left">
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>lake</cell><cell>species</cell><cell>n</cell><cell>mean mass (g)</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>Bogoria</cell><cell>Greater Flamingo</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>2911.8</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Bogoria</cell><cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell><cell>111</cell><cell>1651.6</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Elementaita</cell><cell>Greater Flamingo</cell><cell>28</cell><cell>3152.1</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Elementaita</cell><cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell><cell>37</cell><cell>1777.4</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Nakuru</cell><cell>Greater Flamingo</cell><cell>25</cell><cell>3167.8</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Nakuru</cell><cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell><cell>125</cell><cell>1696.2</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warning-report-group-sizes">
      <title>Always report the group sizes</title>
      <p>
        The greater flamingos at Bogoria average 2912 g against 3168 g at
        Nakuru, a difference of 256 g that might look like a real between-lake
        effect.
      </p>
      <p>
        But <m>n = 10</m> at Bogoria. With a within-species standard deviation
        of roughly 314 g, a sample of ten has a standard error near 100 g, so a
        difference of this size is well within what sampling variation alone
        would produce.
      </p>
      <p>
        A grouped summary table without an <m>n</m> column invites exactly this
        error. Include the counts every time. They are what tell your reader
        which rows to take seriously.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 8: missing data                                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-missing-data">
    <title>Missing Data</title>

    <p>
      We deferred this in <xref ref="ss-first-question" />. It deserves proper
      treatment, because missing values are the norm in real data, and the way
      you handle them silently changes every statistic you compute.
    </p>

    <program language="r">
      colSums(is.na(flamingos))

      #&gt;           lake        species bill_length_mm  bill_depth_mm  leg_length_mm
      #&gt;              0              0              0              4              0
      #&gt;    body_mass_g            sex           year
      #&gt;              8              7              0
    </program>

    <p>
      Eight birds have no recorded mass, four no bill depth, seven no sex. In
      field ecology this is unremarkable: a bird escapes before every
      measurement is taken, equipment fails, a record is illegible.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-why-na">
      <title>Why R returns NA rather than ignoring it</title>

      <p>
        Recall that <c>mean(flamingos$body_mass_g)</c> returned <c>NA</c>. This
        is not R being unhelpful. <c>NA</c> means <q>there is a value here and
        I do not know what it is</q>, and the mean of a set of numbers, one of
        which is unknown, is genuinely unknown. R propagates the uncertainty
        rather than concealing it.
      </p>

      <p>
        This forces you to make an explicit decision. Software that silently
        drops missing values does not remove the decision. It just makes the
        decision for you, invisibly.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-na-rm">
      <title>What na.rm = TRUE actually does</title>

      <p>
        It removes the missing observations and computes on what remains.
        Stated plainly: <em>you are no longer analysing the sample you thought
        you were.</em> Our mean of 1957 g is the mean of 336 birds, not 344.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is essential to be clear that <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> is <em>not</em>
        imputation. It does not estimate the missing values or fill them in. It
        excludes those birds from that calculation entirely.
      </p>

      <p>
        When is dropping safe?
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Reasonably safe</em> when values are missing for reasons
            unrelated to the values themselves <mdash /> a dropped clipboard, a
            smudged record. The remaining birds are then still a fair sample.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Not safe</em> when missingness is related to what you are
            measuring. If the heaviest birds were the hardest to hold still and
            so went unweighed, then dropping them biases your mean downward,
            and no amount of careful computation on the remaining data will
            recover it.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        You usually cannot tell which situation you are in from the data alone.
        It requires knowing how the data was collected. What you can and must
        do is report it: state how many observations were dropped and what
        fraction of the total that represents.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-complete-cases">
      <title>Complete cases and listwise deletion</title>

      <p>
        <c>complete.cases()</c> identifies rows with no missing values
        anywhere.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        sum(complete.cases(flamingos))
        #&gt; [1] 325

        nrow(flamingos)
        #&gt; [1] 344
      </program>

      <p>
        Only 325 of 344 rows are complete: 19 rows, or 5.5%, have at least one
        missing value. Note that this exceeds the 8 missing masses, because the
        gaps fall in different rows for different variables.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dropping every incomplete row, called <term>listwise
        deletion</term><idx><h>listwise deletion</h></idx>, is convenient and
        keeps every analysis on an identical sample, but it discards data
        unnecessarily: a bird missing only its <c>sex</c> still has a perfectly
        good mass measurement. For descriptive work, prefer <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>
        variable by variable, and report the <m>n</m> for each.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="remark-report-n">
        <title>Report the n</title>
        <p>
          Never report a mean, a standard deviation or any other summary
          without also reporting the <m>n</m> it was computed from.
        </p>
        <p>
          In this dataset that alone distinguishes a summary over 344 birds
          from one over 336, over 325, or <mdash /> after grouping by species
          <mdash /> over 63 and 273. Those are different claims about different
          samples, and a table that omits <m>n</m> makes them
          indistinguishable.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 9: R reference section (optional reading)                -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-descriptive-in-r">
    <title>Descriptive Statistics in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This section is a reference. The concepts are developed in
        <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" /> through
        <xref ref="sec-missing-data" />; what follows is the R implementation,
        collected in one place. Work through it at a console with the dataset
        loaded, running each block rather than reading it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where both a base R and a <c>dplyr</c> approach exist, both are shown.
        You should be able to read either, and may write in whichever you
        prefer.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-loading">
      <title>Loading the data</title>

      <program language="r">
        # Set your working directory to wherever the file is saved
        # setwd("~/MIT201")

        flamingos &lt;- read.csv("kenya_flamingos.csv", stringsAsFactors = TRUE)
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>stringsAsFactors = TRUE</c> converts the text columns into
        <term>factors</term>, R's type for categorical data. This matters:
        factors carry a fixed set of levels, which makes <c>table()</c>,
        <c>boxplot()</c> and grouped operations behave sensibly. Since R 4.0.0
        the default is <c>FALSE</c>, so set it explicitly.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-first-look">
      <title>First look at any dataset</title>

      <p>
        Run these four before anything else, every time. They take seconds and
        catch most import errors.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        dim(flamingos)         # rows and columns
        #&gt; [1] 344   8

        names(flamingos)       # variable names

        str(flamingos)         # type and a preview of each variable

        summary(flamingos)     # numeric summaries, plus counts for each factor
      </program>

      <p>
        <c>str()</c> is the most valuable of the four. It tells you whether R
        has read each variable as the type you intended. A numeric column
        silently imported as a factor, usually caused by a stray text entry, is
        one of the most common and most confusing data problems you will meet.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-central-tendency">
      <title>Central tendency</title>

      <program language="r">
        mass &lt;- flamingos$body_mass_g

        mean(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1957.405

        median(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1758.5

        # Mode for a categorical variable
        sort(table(flamingos$species), decreasing = TRUE)
        #&gt; Lesser Flamingo Greater Flamingo
        #&gt;             278               66
      </program>

      <p>
        For a mode of continuous data, bin it first with <c>cut()</c> and take
        the most frequent band, or use <c>density()</c> and find its peak. Note
        again that base R's <c>mode()</c> reports storage type, not the
        statistical mode: <c>mode(mass)</c> returns <c>"numeric"</c>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-dispersion">
      <title>Dispersion and quantiles</title>

      <program language="r">
        range(mass, na.rm = TRUE)      # min and max, not the difference
        #&gt; [1] 1206 3982

        diff(range(mass, na.rm = TRUE))   # the range as a single number
        #&gt; [1] 2776

        var(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 362503.5

        sd(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 602.0827

        IQR(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 384

        quantile(mass, na.rm = TRUE)   # default: 0, 25, 50, 75, 100%

        fivenum(mass)                  # Tukey five-number summary
      </program>

      <p>
        A subtlety worth knowing: there are several defensible definitions of a
        sample quantile when the requested position falls between two
        observations. R implements nine, selectable via the <c>type</c>
        argument, and defaults to <c>type = 7</c>. Other software may default
        differently, so small discrepancies between R and, say, a spreadsheet
        are usually this rather than an error.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-tables">
      <title>Frequency tables and cross-tabulation</title>

      <program language="r">
        table(flamingos$lake)

        table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species)     # cross-tabulation

        xtabs(~ lake + species, data = flamingos)    # same, formula interface

        # Row proportions (margin = 1), column proportions (margin = 2)
        prop.table(table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species), margin = 1)
        prop.table(table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species), margin = 2)

        # With row and column totals
        addmargins(table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species))
      </program>

      <p>
        By default <c>table()</c> excludes missing values silently. Since our
        <c>sex</c> variable has 7 NAs, this matters.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        table(flamingos$sex)
        #&gt; female   male
        #&gt;    170    167
        #&gt; (sums to 337, not 344)

        table(flamingos$sex, useNA = "ifany")   # make the missing values visible
        #&gt; female   male   &lt;NA&gt;
        #&gt;    170    167      7
      </program>

      <p>
        Get into the habit of <c>useNA = "ifany"</c> when you first inspect a
        categorical variable. A frequency table that quietly omits 7 birds is
        exactly the kind of small discrepancy that goes unnoticed until your
        totals stop matching.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-grouped">
      <title>Grouped summaries</title>

      <p>
        In base R:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        # tapply: one summary function, one grouping variable
        tapply(flamingos$body_mass_g, flamingos$species, mean, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;         3120.190         1689.070

        # aggregate: formula interface, extends to several grouping variables
        aggregate(body_mass_g ~ species, data = flamingos, FUN = mean)

        aggregate(body_mass_g ~ lake + species, data = flamingos, FUN = mean)

        # by(): a full summary() per group
        by(flamingos$body_mass_g, flamingos$species, summary)
      </program>

      <p>
        Be aware that <c>aggregate()</c> with a formula drops rows with missing
        values in the modelled variables before it starts, so it silently uses
        336 birds here, not 344. That is usually what you want, but you should
        know it is happening.
      </p>

      <p>
        With <c>dplyr</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(dplyr)

        flamingos %&gt;%
          group_by(species) %&gt;%
          summarise(
            n      = sum(!is.na(body_mass_g)),
            mean   = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
            sd     = sd(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
            median = median(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
            IQR    = IQR(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
          )

        #&gt; # A tibble: 2 x 6
        #&gt;   species              n  mean    sd median   IQR
        #&gt;   &lt;fct&gt;            &lt;int&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;  &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt;
        #&gt; 1 Greater Flamingo    63 3120.  314.  3102.  326.
        #&gt; 2 Lesser Flamingo    273 1689.  196.  1684.  307.
      </program>

      <p>
        Note <c>n = sum(!is.na(body_mass_g))</c> rather than <c>n()</c>. The
        latter counts rows in the group including those with missing mass,
        which would not match the <m>n</m> your mean was actually computed
        from. Grouping by two variables is the same call with
        <c>group_by(lake, species)</c>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-missing">
      <title>Handling missing values</title>

      <program language="r">
        sum(is.na(flamingos$body_mass_g))    # count NAs in one variable
        #&gt; [1] 8

        colSums(is.na(flamingos))            # count NAs in every variable

        sum(complete.cases(flamingos))       # rows with no NAs anywhere
        #&gt; [1] 325

        flamingos_complete &lt;- na.omit(flamingos)   # listwise deletion
        nrow(flamingos_complete)
        #&gt; [1] 325

        # Which rows are incomplete?
        flamingos[!complete.cases(flamingos), ]
      </program>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-plots">
      <title>Visualisation</title>

      <p>
        Base graphics:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        hist(flamingos$body_mass_g, breaks = 20,
             main = "Body mass of Rift Valley flamingos",
             xlab = "Body mass (g)", col = "grey85")

        boxplot(body_mass_g ~ species, data = flamingos,
                ylab = "Body mass (g)", xlab = "")

        plot(density(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
             main = "Density of flamingo body mass")

        plot(body_mass_g ~ bill_length_mm, data = flamingos,
             xlab = "Bill length (mm)", ylab = "Body mass (g)")

        barplot(table(flamingos$lake), ylab = "Number of birds")
      </program>

      <p>
        With <c>ggplot2</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        library(ggplot2)

        # The whole argument of Section 1 in a single plot
        ggplot(flamingos, aes(x = body_mass_g, fill = species)) +
          geom_histogram(bins = 30, alpha = 0.7, position = "identity") +
          geom_vline(xintercept = 1957.4, linetype = "dashed") +
          labs(x = "Body mass (g)", y = "Count",
               title = "Two species, two distributions") +
          theme_minimal()

        ggplot(flamingos, aes(x = species, y = body_mass_g)) +
          geom_boxplot() +
          labs(x = NULL, y = "Body mass (g)") +
          theme_minimal()
      </program>

      <p>
        The first of these is worth running carefully. Colouring the histogram
        by species makes the entire argument of
        <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" /> visible in a single image: two
        clearly separated humps, with the overall mean falling in the trough
        between them.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-r-reference">
      <title>Function reference</title>

      <table xml:id="table-r-function-reference">
        <title>Descriptive statistics functions in R</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Task</cell><cell>Base R</cell><cell>dplyr / tidyverse</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>Mean</cell><cell><c>mean(x, na.rm = TRUE)</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Median</cell><cell><c>median(x, na.rm = TRUE)</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Mode (categorical)</cell><cell><c>names(sort(table(x), decreasing = TRUE))[1]</c></cell><cell><c>count(df, var, sort = TRUE)</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Variance / SD</cell><cell><c>var(x)</c>, <c>sd(x)</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Range</cell><cell><c>range(x)</c>, <c>diff(range(x))</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>IQR</cell><cell><c>IQR(x, na.rm = TRUE)</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Quantiles</cell><cell><c>quantile(x, probs, na.rm = TRUE)</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Overall summary</cell><cell><c>summary(x)</c></cell><cell><c>skimr::skim(df)</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Frequency table</cell><cell><c>table(x)</c></cell><cell><c>count(df, var)</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Cross-tabulation</cell><cell><c>table(x, y)</c>, <c>xtabs(~ x + y)</c></cell><cell><c>count(df, x, y)</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Proportions</cell><cell><c>prop.table(tab, margin)</c></cell><cell><c>mutate(prop = n / sum(n))</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Bin a numeric variable</cell><cell><c>cut(x, breaks)</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Grouped summary</cell><cell><c>tapply()</c>, <c>aggregate()</c></cell><cell><c>group_by()</c> then <c>summarise()</c></cell></row>
          <row><cell>Count missing</cell><cell><c>sum(is.na(x))</c>, <c>colSums(is.na(df))</c></cell><cell>same</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Complete rows</cell><cell><c>complete.cases(df)</c>, <c>na.omit(df)</c></cell><cell><c>drop_na(df)</c></cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-correlation-trap">
      <title>The same trap, in a different disguise</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" /> showed a mean going wrong on
        pooled data. The identical problem afflicts measures of relationship,
        often more severely. Consider the correlation between bill length and
        body mass.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        cor(flamingos$bill_length_mm, flamingos$body_mass_g, use = "complete.obs")
        #&gt; [1] 0.8960

        # Now within each species separately
        by(flamingos, flamingos$species, function(d)
           cor(d$bill_length_mm, d$body_mass_g, use = "complete.obs"))

        #&gt; flamingos$species: Greater Flamingo
        #&gt; [1] 0.1179
        #&gt; flamingos$species: Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt; [1] 0.3230
      </program>

      <p>
        Pooled, the correlation is 0.90, strong enough to look like a firm
        biological law that longer-billed flamingos are heavier. Within species
        it collapses to 0.12 and 0.32.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pooled figure is almost entirely an artefact. Greater flamingos
        have both longer bills and greater mass than lesser flamingos, so
        pooling the two species creates two widely separated clouds of points.
        A straight line through both clouds fits well, but it is measuring the
        gap <em>between</em> species, not any relationship <em>within</em>
        either one.
      </p>

      <p>
        Plot it and the artefact becomes obvious.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        ggplot(flamingos, aes(bill_length_mm, body_mass_g)) +
          geom_point(aes(colour = species), alpha = 0.7) +
          geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE, colour = "black") +
          geom_smooth(aes(colour = species), method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
          theme_minimal()
      </program>

      <p>
        The black line cuts steeply across both clusters; the two coloured
        lines within each cluster are close to flat. This effect, where a
        relationship in pooled data reverses or vanishes once you account for a
        grouping variable, is closely related to <term>Simpson's
        paradox</term><idx><h>Simpson's paradox</h></idx>. It is among the most
        consequential traps in applied statistics, and you will meet it again.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-descriptive-statistics">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-bill-depth-summary">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute the mean, median, standard deviation and IQR of
          <c>bill_depth_mm</c>, first for all birds and then separately by
          species. This variable has 4 missing values <mdash /> report the
          <m>n</m> for every figure you produce.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-bill-depth-interpretation">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Lesser flamingos have deeper bills than greaters despite being the
          smaller bird. Verify this from your answer to
          <xref ref="ex-bill-depth-summary" />, and explain why a pooled mean
          bill depth would be misleading here in a different way from the body
          mass case in <xref ref="sec-flamingo-question" />.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-lake-sex-crosstab">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Build a cross-tabulation of <c>lake</c> against <c>sex</c>, using
          <c>useNA = "ifany"</c>. Convert it to row proportions. Does sex
          composition appear to vary by lake, and does anything about the
          missing values concern you?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-leg-length-grouped">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Produce a grouped summary of <c>leg_length_mm</c> by species
          <em>and</em> sex, including <m>n</m>. Which of the two grouping
          variables accounts for more of the variation?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-bill-length-bands">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Bin <c>bill_length_mm</c> into 5 mm bands with <c>cut()</c> and
          cross-tabulate against species. At what bill length do the two
          species stop overlapping?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-year-trend">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The dataset spans 2021 to 2023. Compute mean body mass by year within
          each species. Is there any evidence of a trend, and given the group
          sizes, would you be willing to claim one?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

<!-- =================================================================
     STACK EXERCISES

     PreTeXt has native support for STACK questions. See the PreTeXt
     Guide, "STACK Exercises" (Overview 3.26, Topics 4.31), for the
     markup. Assessed questions can therefore live in this same source
     file rather than being maintained separately in Moodle.

     Suggested coverage, based on the sections above:

       * Interpretation over computation. Give a mean and a median and
         ask what the gap implies about the distribution, rather than
         asking for a mean to be computed from five numbers.
       * The pooling trap. Present a grouped summary table and a pooled
         one, and ask which is appropriate for a stated question.
       * Reading a contingency table. Supply row and column proportions
         of the same table and ask which answers a given question.
       * Missing data. Give a summary reporting n = 336 from a dataset
         of 344 and ask what must be stated alongside it.
       * Choosing a plot. Given a question about the data, ask which of
         histogram, boxplot, scatter or bar chart would answer it,
         including a case where a boxplot would hide the answer.
       * Randomised computation. Where calculation is genuinely wanted,
         cut() band boundaries and quantile probabilities randomise
         cleanly across variants.
     ================================================================= -->

</chapter>
